= 3
{parent=2010}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $\Delta T=T_0-T_B$, $\Delta C=C_0-C_B$ and assume these scales are nonzero. The two reference <liquidus> relations imply $\Delta T=-m\Delta C$. For the <similarity variable> $\eta=z/(2\sqrt{\kappa t})$ and front $h=2\lambda\sqrt{\kappa t}$,
$$
\eta_t=-\frac{\eta}{2t},\quad\eta_z=\frac1{2\sqrt{\kappa t}},\quad h'=\lambda\sqrt{\kappa/t}.
$$
Thus a <temperature> profile $T=T_*+\Delta T\theta(\eta)$ has $T_t=-\Delta T\eta\theta'/(2t)$ and $\kappa T_{zz}=\Delta T\theta''/(4t)$. Each <heat equation> becomes $\theta''+2\eta\theta'=0$. The solute <diffusion equation> becomes $\mathcal C^{l\prime\prime}+2Le\eta\mathcal C^{l\prime}=0$, with
$$
\boxed{Le=\kappa/D.}
$$
This <Lewis number> compares thermal and solutal <diffusivities>. Inside the <mushy layer>, $T^s-T_B=-m(C^s-C_B)$ gives $\boxed{\mathcal C^s=\theta^s}$. The wall and far-field conditions become $\theta^s(0)=0$ and $\theta^l,\mathcal C^l\to0$ as $\eta\to\infty$.

At the moving <phase boundary>, equality of <temperatures> gives $\theta^s=\theta^l+(T_\infty-T_B)/\Delta T$. Equality of <concentrations> gives $\mathcal C^s=\mathcal C^l+1$. Therefore the dimensionless parameters with the convention used here are
$$
\boxed{\mathcal R_\theta=\frac{T_0-T_\infty}{T_0-T_B},\qquad \mathcal R_C=\frac{C_0}{C_0-C_B},\qquad S=\frac{\rho L\kappa}{k(T_0-T_B)}.}
$$
Neither <concentration> nor <temperature> offsets should be dropped when enforcing continuity. In particular $\mathcal R_\theta$ can be negative. If $k=\rho c_p\kappa$, then $S=L/[c_p\Delta T]$: this is the <inverse Stefan number>, although the paper calls $S$ its Stefan number.

To check every factor of two in the flux conditions, divide the thermal <Stefan condition> by $k\Delta T/(2\sqrt{\kappa t})$, and the solute balance by $D\Delta C/(2\sqrt{\kappa t})$. This gives
$$
\boxed{2\phi\lambda S=\theta^{s\prime}-\theta^{l\prime},\qquad 2\phi Le\lambda(\mathcal C^l+\mathcal R_C)=-\mathcal C^{l\prime}.}
$$
Finally $T_z^l=-mC_z^l$ and $-m\Delta C=\Delta T$ imply $\boxed{\theta^{l\prime}=\mathcal C^{l\prime}}$. This is <marginal equilibrium at a mush-liquid boundary>. Together these calculations produce all the rescaled differential equations and all three interface balances.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Integrating $Y''+2a\eta Y'=0$ once gives $Y'=K e^{-a\eta^2}$. A second integration expresses the solution using the <error function> when the value at zero is prescribed, and the <complementary error function> when decay at infinity is prescribed. Write $\theta_\lambda=\theta^s(\lambda)$ and take $\lambda>0$, $Le>0$. The wall condition and the interface value give
$$
\boxed{\theta^s(\eta)=\mathcal C^s(\eta)=\theta_\lambda\frac{\operatorname{erf}\eta}{\operatorname{erf}\lambda},\qquad0\leq\eta\leq\lambda.}
$$
For the exterior liquid, continuity at the interface fixes $\theta^l(\lambda)=\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_\theta$ and $\mathcal C^l(\lambda)=\theta_\lambda-1$. Thus the two decaying <similarity solutions> are
$$
\boxed{\theta^l(\eta)=(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_\theta)\frac{\operatorname{erfc}\eta}{\operatorname{erfc}\lambda},\qquad
\mathcal C^l(\eta)=(\theta_\lambda-1)\frac{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{Le}\eta)}{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{Le}\lambda)},\quad\eta\geq\lambda.}
$$
The factor $\sqrt{Le}$ is required because solute and heat diffuse on different length scales. Both denominators are positive for a finite positive $\lambda$. These formulas use the differential equations, wall/far-field data and continuity; the remaining three flux conditions determine their unknown constants.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Introduce three positive functions of the unknown front parameter:
$$
P(\lambda)=\frac{e^{-\lambda^2}}{\sqrt\pi\operatorname{erf}\lambda},\quad
Q(\lambda)=\frac{e^{-\lambda^2}}{\sqrt\pi\operatorname{erfc}\lambda},\quad
R(\lambda)=\frac{\sqrt{Le}\,e^{-Le\lambda^2}}{\sqrt\pi\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{Le}\lambda)}.
$$
Differentiating the <error function> profiles gives $\theta^{s\prime}=2P\theta_\lambda$, $\theta^{l\prime}=-2Q(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_\theta)$ and $\mathcal C^{l\prime}=-2R(\theta_\lambda-1)$ at the interface. The three flux conditions are therefore exactly
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\phi\lambda S&=P\theta_\lambda+Q(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_\theta),\\
\phi Le\lambda(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_C)&=R(\theta_\lambda-1),\\
Q(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_\theta)&=R(\theta_\lambda-1).
\end{aligned}}
$$
These undivided equations are the safest description of the <constant-fraction mushy-layer similarity solution>, including degenerate parameter choices. In the nondegenerate case the third equation first gives
$$
\boxed{\theta_\lambda=1+\frac{Q(\lambda)\mathcal R_\theta}{R(\lambda)-Q(\lambda)}.}
$$
The second then gives the <solid fraction>
$$
\boxed{\phi=\frac{R(\lambda)(\theta_\lambda-1)}{Le\lambda(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_C)}.}
$$
Use the third equation to replace the $Q$ term in the first, and eliminate $\phi$. The front parameter is a positive root of the single <transcendental equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac{S R(\lambda)(\theta_\lambda-1)}{Le(\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_C)}-P(\lambda)\theta_\lambda-R(\lambda)(\theta_\lambda-1)=0,\qquad
\theta_\lambda=1+\frac{Q(\lambda)\mathcal R_\theta}{R(\lambda)-Q(\lambda)}.}
$$
Then substitute back for $\theta_\lambda,\phi$ and $h(t)=2\lambda\sqrt{\kappa t}$. Since the physical parameters have not been assigned numbers, an implicit scalar equation is the appropriate determination of $\lambda$.

The divided formulas require $R\ne Q$ and $\theta_\lambda-1+\mathcal R_C\ne0$. If either vanishes, return to the boxed three-equation system. For example $Le=1$ gives $R=Q$, so the last equation demands $\mathcal R_\theta=0$; otherwise this proposed <similarity solution> cannot exist. A physical branch must also have $\lambda>0$ and $0\leq\phi\leq1$. The algebra alone does not establish existence or uniqueness of such a branch for arbitrary parameters.
